<article class="env-lemma" id="07D1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07D1">Lemma <span data-tag="07D1">15.34.3</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $g_1, \ldots , g_ m \in S$ generate the unit ideal. If each $R \to S_{g_ j}$ is a local complete intersection so is $R \to S$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $S = R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/I$ be a presentation. Pick $h_ j \in R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ mapping to $g_ j$ in $S$. Then $R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n, x_{n + 1}]/(I, x_{n + 1}h_ j - 1)$ is a presentation of $S_{g_ j}$. Hence $I_ j = (I, x_{n + 1}h_ j - 1)$ is a Koszul-regular ideal in $R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n, x_{n + 1}]$. Pick a prime $I \subset \mathfrak q \subset R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$. Then $h_ j \not\in \mathfrak q$ for some $j$ and $\mathfrak q_ j = (\mathfrak q, x_{n + 1}h_ j - 1)$ is a prime ideal of $V(I_ j)$ lying over $\mathfrak q$. Pick $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in I$ which map to a basis of $I/I^2 \otimes \kappa (\mathfrak q)$. Then $x_{n + 1}h_ j - 1, f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is a sequence of elements of $I_ j$ which map to a basis of $I_ j \otimes \kappa (\mathfrak q_ j)$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/08JZ" data-tag="08JZ">10.134.12</a>. By Nakayama's lemma there exists an $h \in R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n, x_{n + 1}]$ such that $(I_ j)_ h$ is generated by $x_{n + 1}h_ j - 1, f_1, \ldots , f_ r$. We may also assume that $(I_ j)_ h$ is generated by a Koszul regular sequence of some length $e$. Looking at the dimension of $I_ j \otimes \kappa (\mathfrak q_ j)$ we see that $e = r + 1$. Hence by Lemma <a href="/tag/066A" data-tag="066A">15.31.15</a> we see that $x_{n + 1}h_ j - 1, f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is a Koszul-regular sequence generating $(I_ j)_ h$ for some $h \in R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n, x_{n + 1}]$, $h \not\in \mathfrak q_ j$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/068M" data-tag="068M">15.31.14</a> we see that $I_{h'}$ is generated by a Koszul-regular sequence for some $h' \in R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$, $h' \not\in \mathfrak q$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>